  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nFriday through Saturday Night...\n\nAs we head into the weekend, the latest suite of model data shows \nbroad upper level troughing remaining in place across the Midwest \nand into the Great Lakes.  Corresponding ridging will be seen across \nthe western CONUS and into the western Rockies.  A diffuse surface \nfrontal boundary is expected to stall out north of the region on \nFriday and into Saturday.  The LMK CWA will remain on the south side \nof this boundary with PWATs remaining in the 1.7-1.9 inch range. \nAloft, multiple perturbations are expected to move through the region \nand will bring episodic bouts of convection.  Model soundings show \nenough instability to support somewhat organized convection. \nHowever, shear values will remain quite weak with speeds of less \nthan 20kts.  The risk for severe weather during this period \ncontinues to be on the low side.  However, that high PWAT air will \nlikely lead to precipitation loading in storms with torrential \nrainfall and localized gusty winds with any stronger storm.  Slow \nenvironmental flow could result in excessive rainfall, especially in \nareas that see repeated convection.  Highs Friday and Saturday will \nbe in the 85-90 degree range with overnight lows in the upper \n60s/lower 70s.\n\nSunday through Wednesday...\n\nMoving into the later part of the weekend, the upper level pattern \nlooks to deamplify slightly as the upper trough axis over the Great \nLakes moves easts and the flow aloft becomes a bit more zonal. \nStronger westerly flow aloft will be confined to the northern tier \nof the US which should result in a storm track further to our north \nwith lesser rain chances across the Ohio Valley for Monday and \nTuesday.   Highs Sunday will warm again into the 85-90 degree range. \nFor Monday and Tuesday, we'll a slight increase in 1000-500 hPa \nthickness which should support afternoon temps in the upper 80s to \nthe lower 90s.  Temps could be slightly cooler here as a number of \nconvective systems will likely move across the northern Midwest and \nGreat Lakes through the period and could produce some high clouds \nacross the region limiting heating.\n\nBy mid-week, the upper level ridge across the western CONUS is \nexpected amplify with downstream troughing redeveloping across the \neast.  The flow pattern across the Ohio Valley will develop into a \nnotorious northwest flow pattern.  Several perturbations are likely \nto drop southeast into the region resulting in episodic bouts of \nconvection and possible MCS activity with excessive rainfall \nbecoming a problem by late week.",
